---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import GitCommandBuilder from '../../components/GitCommandBuilder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Git Command Cheat Sheet" description="Click-to-copy reference for common git commands — undo, branching, history, and stashing.">
  <GitCommandBuilder client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why the same handful of commands cover most days</h2>
  <p>Despite git's huge surface area, day-to-day work relies on a fairly small set of commands repeated constantly — undoing a mistake, checking history, and managing branches. Having them one click away saves the mental overhead of recalling exact syntax and flags.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the command matching your current task.</li>
    <li>Click to copy, then run it in your terminal, adjusting placeholders as needed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using a destructive command without understanding its scope</strong> — commands like reset --hard permanently discard changes with no built-in confirmation.</li>
    <li><strong>Not checking <code>git status</code> first</strong> before running an unfamiliar command, missing context about the current state of the working directory.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly recalling the exact syntax for an infrequently used command</li>
    <li>Onboarding someone newer to git with a curated set of essentials</li>
    <li>Having a fast reference open alongside your terminal during a tricky git situation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this a complete git reference?</strong> No — it covers the most commonly needed day-to-day commands; git's full command set is much larger and covered by its official documentation.</p>
</ToolLayout>
